# Chess Game

A command-line chess game written in Java with full rule implementation.

## Features
- All piece movements (pawns, knights, bishops, rooks, queens, kings)
- Special moves: castling, en passant, pawn promotion
- Check, checkmate, and stalemate detection
- Move validation (prevents illegal moves including leaving king in check)
- Full undo/redo support with castle rights restoration
- Move history tracking

## How to Run
1. Run: `java Main`
2. Enter moves in algebraic notation (e.g., `move e2 e4`)

## Commands
- `move [from] [to]` - Move a piece (e.g., `move e2 e4`)
  - For pawn promotion, you'll be prompted to choose a piece (knight/bishop/rook/queen)
- `show` - Display the current board
- `showMoves` - List all legal moves for the current player
- `undo` - Undo the last move
- `redo` - Redo a previously undone move
- `quit` - Exit the game

## Technical Details
- Board representation: 2D integer array with signed values for piece colors
- Move generation: Pseudo-legal moves filtered by check validation
- Algorithm: Iterates through all pieces to generate candidate moves, applies each temporarily to verify king safety

## Known limitations
- doesn't detect threefold repetition
- doesn't detect 50-move rule
- doesn't detect insufficient material

## Future Improvements
- Chess engine with minimax/alpha-beta pruning
- Opening book integration
- Bitboard representation for performance
- GUI using JavaFX
- Convert 2D board array to 1D array for engine performance
- Use ArrayList instead of fixed-size Move arrays
- Use bitmask for castle rights instead of 4 separate booleans
- Implement proper equals() and hashCode() for Move class
- Return defensive copy of board in getChessBoard()
- Extract magic numbers to named constants (rook castle columns, square counts)

## What I Learned
- Game state management and move validation
- Working with complex rule sets
- Debugging edge cases in special moves
- Balancing code readability with performance considerations